// Central classification of every `pending*Choice` prompt: is it OPTIONAL, and is it BENEFICIAL or
// HARMFUL? Task #521, 2026-07-25, from a playtest report that mandatory effects could be skipped.
//
// WHY A TABLE RATHER THAN A FIELD ON EACH CARD. 107 registrations across 6 prompt families needed
// classifying at once. Spread over ~100 card files this would be unreviewable and un-revisable: the
// project owner could not see the whole ruling in one place, and correcting a mis-call would mean
// hunting for the file. This is a classification problem, not per-card logic, so it lives in one
// table. Cards that need genuinely DYNAMIC optionality (optional only in certain states) still win:
// `resolvePendingChoiceMeta` prefers whatever the card's own prompt function returned.
//
// THE DEFAULT IS MANDATORY, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. Anything absent from this table is treated as
// mandatory, so its Skip/Cancel button disappears. Erring that way is safe: a mis-classified
// mandatory prompt forces a choice the player could have declined -- visible and reportable -- while
// a mis-classified OPTIONAL one silently restores the exploit (click Skip, dodge the drawback).
//
// HOW A CARD EARNS `optional: true`: the printed text must say "may" (or "up to", or "as you wish")
// ABOUT THIS CHOICE. A "may" elsewhere on the card does not count -- most of these cards have
// several clauses and only some are optional. Every entry below was read against the card's own
// printed text in `cards.json`, not inferred from the hook body: where a hook and the text disagree,
// the TEXT is authoritative and the hook is the thing that needs fixing.
//
// `intent` is cosmetic only -- it orders the dropdown so the likeliest target comes first (own cards
// first, smallest first, for a beneficial effect; opponents first, biggest first, for a harmful
// one). It never changes what is legal. LEAVE IT UNSET when a prompt is neither, or when the pool is
// deliberately the controller's OWN cards for a harmful effect (Olum Mystic's Plague makes each
// player damage their own Creature -- sorting opponents first there would actively mislead).

