// The headless driver (task #583, 2026-07-29) -- the first consumer of the three read-side seams
// added by #580/#581/#582, and the thing the fuzz harness, the bot and a network peer will all sit
// on. See docs/plans/phase-next-pvp-and-bot.md, Phase 1.
//
// It plays a whole game with no React involved, through the SAME engine functions Play.tsx calls.
// Deliberately DUMB (the plan's 1a): it picks uniformly at random from what the engine says is
// legal. It is not trying to play well, and it never will be -- the bot's evaluator is a separate
// policy layered on the same interface, not a rewrite of this.
//
// THE LOOP, in this order:
//   1. discharge obligations until getPendingObligations is empty
//   2. pick a move from getLegalMoves
//   3. loop describeMoveChoices until it returns [], then apply
// Obligations come first because several of them are the engine mid-way through resolving something
// else (the naming and sacrifice sequences, the pre-defeat interrupt).
//
// TWO REFUSAL RULES, both load-bearing, both inherited from #580/#582:
//   * A `{ kind: 'unsupported' }` choice means the driver must NOT play the move. Card files read
//     choices as `chosenAmounts?.[id] ?? cap`, so omitting one does not crash -- it silently plays
//     the maximum. A fuzzer doing that explores a fraction of the choice space while reporting full
//     coverage. Refuse, record, move on to a different move.
//   * A skipped obligation does not stall either; it fizzles (swept at the next advancePhase) or
//     sits unanswered forever. Same invisibility. Hence step 1 above.
// Both refusals are RECORDED rather than thrown. The record is the work queue: which mechanisms to
// teach the driver next, in frequency order, derived from real games instead of from guesses.
//
// DETERMINISM, and the ONE subtlety in this file. There are TWO seeded streams, and they must stay
// separate:
//   * engineRng -- passed into engine calls. Shuffles, die rolls, anything the RULES randomise.
//   * policyRng -- the driver's own "which move do I take" picks.
// Both derive from `seed`, so a game is still reproducible from seed + the two deck lists.
//
// A single shared stream is WRONG, and it is wrong in a way that only the replay oracle catches.
// Play.tsx's human consumes no rng at all when they decide something; only the engine draws from
// gameRngRef. A driver that draws its own picks from that same stream advances it, so replaying the
// recorded log -- which re-runs ONLY the engine calls, against a fresh createSeededRng(seed) --
// finds every subsequent shuffle landing differently. The first build of this file did exactly
// that, and test/driver.test.ts's replay case failed with "Illegal play: Card is not in hand": by
// replay time the card the driver had played was somewhere else in a differently-shuffled deck.
// Fixed by splitting the streams. Recorded because the symptom points at the engine and the cause
// is in the harness.
//
// `resetInstanceCounter()` inside setupGame is what stops instance ids drifting between runs.

