// Urhail -- top-level text: "Underneath Shadow Magi may play Urhail, ignoring regional penalties and
// restrictions." -- a data grant handled by the database's own `shadow_play` field + regions.ts, no
// code needed. "Tainted Dreams: Your Magi may play Core Spells, ignoring regional penalties and
// restrictions." -- registry.ts's `regionOverride`, same shape as Sorrowing Ogar's / Sand Hyren's
// "may play Core cards ignoring restrictions", but scoped to `card_type === 'Spell'`.
//
// Built task #48, 2026-07-18, the Underneath region pass -- Category A. Buildable NOW even though a
// Spell's own resolution does nothing yet: Spells ARE playable today (play.ts's `isLegalToPlay`
// region-checks them and broadcasts `regionOverride` over the acting player's Magi + in-play cards
// for ANY card type, then playCard resolves the Spell straight to the discard pile), so relaxing a
// Core-Spell region restriction is a real, reachable effect. Distinct from Orlon (deferred), which
// needs opposing Spells to RESOLVE an effect that could be prevented. `regionOverride` only scans
// the acting player's own Magi + in-play cards, so "your Magi" is satisfied automatically (Urhail
// only contributes while it is its controller's own in-play Creature). Corrects the stale
// index.ts note (2026-07-12) that wrongly claimed Urhail "needs no code" -- that survey saw only the
// shadow_play line and missed Tainted Dreams entirely.

// Ashio's Weave Twist (2026-07-24): "Core" above is Urhail's OWN printed text, so under Ashio it
// means its own region (Underneath) instead -- a no-op in effect, since Underneath Magi already
// play Underneath Spells natively. See targeting.ts's ashioAdjustedRegion doc comment.

import { registerCardEffect } from './registry.js';
import { ashioAdjustedRegion } from '../targeting.js';

registerCardEffect('Urhail', {
  regionOverride: (state, cardDb, playedDef) =>
    playedDef.card_type === 'Spell' && playedDef.regions.includes(ashioAdjustedRegion(state, cardDb, 'Core', ['Underneath'])),
});
