// Tomes of the Great Library -- "Research (1): Search your deck or any opposing discard pile for
// any one Spell. Play the chosen Spell, ignoring regional restrictions. If that Spell came from
// your deck, discard it. If it came from an opposing discard pile, shuffle it into its owner's
// deck." Plus the passive text: "Tomes of the Great Library cannot be discarded from play by
// opposing cards that specifically discard Relics from play. Discard Tomes of the Great Library
// if your Magi is not a Naroom Magi or Naroom Shadow Magi."
//
// Full state after the 2026-08-13 deferred-list re-audit (multi-region sub-session): all four
// clauses are built.
//   1. Search-own-deck source: `deckSearch` below (long-standing).
//   2. Search-opposing-discard source: `discardPileSearch` with `whoseDiscardPile: 'opposing'`
//      below (Junjertrug Horn shape -- combined picker with a shared count-1 budget; the header
//      used to claim only the deck source was implemented, but the discard-pile source was
//      actually shipped 2026-07-25 as part of the same `whoseDiscardPile` primitive that Tidal
//      Karak/Junjertrug Horn use, and re-verified live below. The old "would force a mandatory
//      pick" concern was moot once `deckSearch`+`discardPileSearch` on one Power became the
//      declared shape).
//   3. Relic-discard immunity: `canBeDiscardedFromPlay: () => false` -- Kybar's Fang's exact
//      pattern for "cannot be discarded from play by opposing cards that specifically discard
//      Relics from play." Only consulted from `effects.ts#discardFromPlay` (the explicit path),
//      which is exactly where a Relic-specific-discard card routes, so the blunt shape is
//      correct (see registry.ts's own `canBeDiscardedFromPlay` doc comment on why: "they can
//      only ever block a *specific* discard, which is what their cards say.")
//   4. "Discard if your Magi is not Naroom": `applyRegionLockedRelic` shared helper (twin of
//      Kybar's Fang, Vault of Knowledge Key, Gift of the Weave, Gate to Ombor), checked at the
//      start of the controller's own turn. `regions.includes('Naroom')` on `magiDef` covers
//      both Naroom Magi (regions is exactly ['Naroom']) and a Naroom Magi currently in Shadow
//      form (Hrada's Tainted Touch is a runtime state that does not mutate the card's printed
//      `regions`, so 'Naroom' stays in the definition -- same reasoning every other regional
//      Magi-check in this file family relies on).
//
// CORRECTED 2026-07-21 (task #53's Core Spell pass, Heart of the Sky follow-up): the searched Spell
// used to go straight to the discard pile with NONE of its printed effects ever applying --
// `effects.ts`'s `playFromZone` had the same "Spells never execute" staleness bug already found and
// fixed in `forcePlayFromHand`/`castSpellFromOpponentZone`. Now fixed at the shared `playFromZone`
// level, so the searched Spell here genuinely resolves before being discarded (or stays in play, for
// a `spellStaysInPlay` Spell) -- no change needed in this file beyond passing `ctx.rng` through so a
// searched Spell that itself rolls dice/needs a Rng uses this game's seeded one, not the default.
//
// CORRECTED AGAIN 2026-07-21 (playtest bug report): a `spellStaysInPlay` Spell with its own
// sub-cost (e.g. Naroom/Orothe Shadow Geyser's "discard 3 of your Core Creatures/Relics from play")
// used to enter play completely for FREE via this Power -- `playFromZone` used to force-place any
// `spellStaysInPlay` Spell straight into play, skipping `resolveSpell` (and therefore its cost)
// entirely. Fixed at `playFromZone`'s own level: `resolveSpell` is now always called, so a searched
// Geyser's own cost-choice fields (`chosenInPlayDiscardCostIds`/`chosenHandCardIds`) are checked
// same as normal -- but since THIS Power has no nested "now also choose which 3 Relics to discard"
// UI step, those fields are always empty here, so the Geyser's own `resolveSpell` correctly does
// nothing and the Spell fizzles to the discard pile instead (same class of gap as this file's own
// "opposing discard pile" deferral above, and as Hand of the Sky's "the stolen Spell's own target is
// never collected" gap) -- an honest fizzle, not a free pass. A `spellStaysInPlay` Spell needing NO
// external choice (Awakening, Cald Shadow Geyser) is unaffected and still enters play correctly.
//
// FIXED FOR REAL 2026-07-21 (task #405, same-day follow-up playtest report: Crushing Darkness found
// via Research resolved -- cost paid, card discarded -- but its own "choose a Creature" target was
// never collected, so it did nothing). The paragraph above only fixed COST choices for
// `spellStaysInPlay` Spells; a found Spell's own TARGET (or hand-discard/boolean/hand-play/
// discard-pile-search/category choice) was still never collected by this Power. Play.tsx's
// `maybeOpenNestedSpellPrompt` now looks ahead at the specific Spell `chosenDeckSearchIds` names,
// opens a SECOND prompt for that Spell's own choices if it needs any, and threads the answer through
// `ctx.nestedSpellChoices` -> `playFromZone`'s new `nestedChoices` param below. No code change
// needed in THIS function beyond passing `ctx.nestedSpellChoices` through.

import { registerCardEffect } from './registry.js';
import { playFromZone, spellPlayCostWithModifiers } from '../effects.js';
import { addToZone, removeFromZone, shuffleDeck } from '../zones.js';
import { isVariableCostPower, resolveVariableCost } from '../power.js';
import { getOpponents } from '../types/state.js';
import { applyRegionLockedRelic } from './regionLockedRelic.js';
import type { CardDatabase } from '../types/card.js';
import type { CardInstance } from '../types/instance.js';
import type { GameState, PlayerId } from '../types/state.js';

/** What this Power will actually charge for a found Spell: printed cost, plus every applicable
 *  `costModifier` (Jasker's Mask's Spell tax, Marella's reductions...) via the shared
 *  `spellPlayCostWithModifiers` -- the same helper `castSpellFromOpponentZone` uses for Hand/Heart
 *  of the Sky, so all three "play a Spell from elsewhere" routes price identically.
 *
 *  An X-cost Spell resolves its X against `chosenXValue`, collected by the nested prompt (2026-07-27).
 *  It used to be hardcoded to 0 here, which cast the whole X-cost family for free.
 *
 *  NO regional penalty is added -- "ignoring regional restrictions" is precisely what that waives. */
function researchBaseCost(cardDb: CardDatabase, card: CardInstance, chosenXValue = 0): number {
  const cost = cardDb.get(card.definitionKey)?.cost;
  if (typeof cost === 'number') return cost;
  // X / X+N (2026-07-27). This used to `return 0` for ANY non-numeric cost, which made every
  // X-cost Spell fetched through Research completely FREE -- measured, not inferred: Syphon Vortex
  // ("X+4") took a 20-energy Magi to 19, i.e. the Power's own (1) and nothing for the Spell.
  // `resolveVariableCost` is power.ts's existing parser, exported specifically so a card's own
  // printed "X+N" cost is read the same way a Power's is; its own doc comment names Syphon Vortex.
  if (isVariableCostPower(cost)) return resolveVariableCost(cost!, chosenXValue);
  return 0;
}

/**
 * The CHEAPEST this Spell could possibly cost, i.e. its cost at X = 0. Used by the search filters,
 * which run BEFORE the player has chosen an X.
 *
 * X-cost Spells (Syphon Vortex "X+4", Stone Storm, Turn, Burrow, Wanderlust...) stay in the pool as
 * long as the Magi could afford the fixed part -- excluding them outright would remove a whole class
 * of card from Research, which is not what "search for any one Spell" says. The player picks first,
 * then the nested prompt asks for X, and `resolvePower` below re-checks affordability against the
 * X they actually chose. Owner ruling 2026-07-27.
 */
function researchMinimumCost(state: GameState, cardDb: CardDatabase, playerId: PlayerId, card: CardInstance): number {
  return researchFinalCost(state, cardDb, playerId, card, 0);
}

/** What will ACTUALLY be charged, i.e. the base above with `costModifier`s applied. Used only for
 *  the affordability checks -- `playFromZone` is handed the BASE and applies the modifiers itself,
 *  so passing this value there instead would apply them twice. */
function researchFinalCost(
  state: GameState,
  cardDb: CardDatabase,
  playerId: PlayerId,
  card: CardInstance,
  chosenXValue = 0
): number {
  const def = cardDb.get(card.definitionKey);
  if (!def) return 0;
  return spellPlayCostWithModifiers(state, cardDb, def, playerId, researchBaseCost(cardDb, card, chosenXValue));
}

registerCardEffect('Tomes of the Great Library', {
  // Clause 3: "cannot be discarded from play by opposing cards that specifically discard Relics
  // from play." Kybar's Fang / Vault of Knowledge Key / Gift of the Weave / Gate to Ombor share
  // this exact shape and use the same blunt `() => false` -- correct because this hook is only
  // consulted from `effects.ts#discardFromPlay` (the explicit path), never from the zero-energy
  // defeat sweep, so a Relic-specific-discard card is exactly what this blocks.
  canBeDiscardedFromPlay: () => false,
  // Clause 4: "Discard Tomes of the Great Library if your Magi is not a Naroom Magi or Naroom
  // Shadow Magi." Standard `applyRegionLockedRelic` twin -- checked at own-turn-start (the same
  // standing-condition cadence Kybar's Fang etc. use). `regions.includes('Naroom')` catches
  // Naroom Shadow Magi too since Shadow conversion is a runtime state that does not mutate the
  // Magi's printed regions list.
  onOwnTurnStart: (state, cardDb, self, rng) =>
    applyRegionLockedRelic(state, cardDb, self, (magiDef) => magiDef.regions.includes('Naroom'), rng),
  deckSearch: {
    count: 1,
    // Only Spells the Magi can actually pay for. Without this the player could pick a Spell they
    // can't afford, and playFromZone would clamp the payment at 0 and hand it to them cheap --
    // re-introducing the very bug this filter exists to close.
    filter: (state, cardDb, playerId, card) => {
      if (cardDb.get(card.definitionKey)?.card_type !== 'Spell') return false;
      const magiEnergy = state.players.get(playerId)?.activeMagi?.currentEnergy ?? 0;
      // Cheapest-possible cost: an X-cost Spell qualifies if the Magi can cover the fixed part.
      // The real X is chosen in the nested prompt and re-checked in resolvePower below.
      return researchMinimumCost(state, cardDb, playerId, card) <= magiEnergy;
    },
  },
  // "…or any opposing discard pile". `whoseDiscardPile: 'opposing'` (new 2026-07-25) reads the one
  // opponent with NO target pick -- 2-player, so there is nothing to choose. Declaring both
  // `deckSearch` and `discardPileSearch` on one Power is the Junjertrug Horn shape: the UI offers a
  // combined picker with a shared count budget (Play.tsx's `hasCombinedDeckAndDiscardSearch`), and
  // "any ONE" is enforced below in resolvePower, exactly as Junjertrug Horn's Summon does.
  discardPileSearch: {
    count: 1,
    whoseDiscardPile: 'opposing',
    filter: (state, cardDb, playerId, card) => {
      if (cardDb.get(card.definitionKey)?.card_type !== 'Spell') return false;
      const magiEnergy = state.players.get(playerId)?.activeMagi?.currentEnergy ?? 0;
      // Cheapest-possible cost: an X-cost Spell qualifies if the Magi can cover the fixed part.
      // The real X is chosen in the nested prompt and re-checked in resolvePower below.
      return researchMinimumCost(state, cardDb, playerId, card) <= magiEnergy;
    },
  },
  resolvePower: (ctx) => {
    if (ctx.powerName !== 'Research') return;
    const deckIds = ctx.chosenDeckSearchIds ?? [];
    const discardIds = ctx.chosenDiscardPileIds ?? [];
    // "any ONE Spell" across BOTH sources -- picking one from each is not a legal choice, so the
    // whole Power fizzles rather than resolving half of it (Junjertrug Horn's own precedent).
    if (deckIds.length + discardIds.length !== 1) return;

    const fromDeck = deckIds.length === 1;
    const id = fromDeck ? deckIds[0] : discardIds[0];
    // The opponent whose pile was searched -- also the "owner" the Spell is shuffled back into
    // below, per "shuffle it into its owner's deck".
    const opponent = getOpponents(ctx.state, ctx.controllingPlayer.playerId)[0];
    const sourceOwner = fromDeck ? ctx.controllingPlayer : opponent;
    if (!sourceOwner) return;
    const card = (fromDeck ? sourceOwner.deck : sourceOwner.discardPile).find((c) => c.instanceId === id);
    if (!card || ctx.cardDb.get(card.definitionKey)?.card_type !== 'Spell') return;
    // Re-checked at resolution, not just at pick time: the Power's own (1) cost is paid between the
    // two, so the affordable pool computed when the picker opened can be stale by now.
    //
    // THE X GUARDRAIL (2026-07-27). The pool filter only proved the Magi could cover the cheapest
    // possible cost (X=0); this is where the X the player actually chose is priced and checked. An
    // unaffordable X fizzles the Power rather than clamping the payment -- clamping is exactly how
    // the original free-play bug behaved. The UI blocks an unaffordable X at Confirm, so reaching
    // this is either a bot/test caller or a board that changed underneath the prompt.
    const chosenXValue = ctx.nestedSpellChoices?.chosenXValue ?? 0;
    const finalCost = researchFinalCost(ctx.state, ctx.cardDb, ctx.controllingPlayer.playerId, card, chosenXValue);
    if (finalCost > (ctx.controllingPlayer.activeMagi?.currentEnergy ?? 0)) return;
    const cost = researchBaseCost(ctx.cardDb, card, chosenXValue); // playFromZone applies the modifiers
    playFromZone(
      ctx.state,
      ctx.cardDb,
      { owner: sourceOwner, zone: fromDeck ? 'deck' : 'discardPile', instanceId: id! },
      ctx.controllingPlayer,
      null,
      ctx.rng,
      ctx.nestedSpellChoices,
      cost
    );

    // "If that Spell came from your deck, discard it. If it came from an opposing discard pile,
    // shuffle it into its owner's deck." playFromZone already handles the deck case -- a resolved
    // Spell that didn't place itself lands in the CASTER's discard pile. For the discard-pile case
    // that destination is wrong, so move it to the original owner's deck and shuffle.
    //
    // KNOWN LIMITATION, matching the rest of this card's honest-fizzle behaviour: the errata'd text
    // says "WHEN THAT SPELL LEAVES PLAY, if that Spell came from…", which only differs for a
    // `spellStaysInPlay` Spell -- one that is still in play when this Power finishes. There is no
    // delayed "when this specific card later leaves play, send it somewhere unusual" mechanism in
    // this engine, so such a Spell is left in play and will go to the caster's discard pile by the
    // normal route rather than back to its owner's deck. For every ordinary Spell (which leaves
    // play immediately as it resolves) the clause below IS the errata'd behaviour.
    if (!fromDeck) {
      // Search BOTH discard piles, not just the caster's: `moveToDiscardPile` routes a card to its
      // OWNER's pile, not the playing player's, so a Spell borrowed from the opponent lands back in
      // the OPPONENT's discard pile. (Assuming the caster's pile here is exactly the mistake that
      // made the first version of this silently do nothing.)
      for (const holder of ctx.state.players.values()) {
        const landed = holder.discardPile.find((c) => c.instanceId === id);
        if (!landed) continue;
        removeFromZone(holder, 'discardPile', landed.instanceId);
        landed.controllerId = sourceOwner.playerId; // back under its owner, not the caster
        addToZone(sourceOwner, 'deck', landed);
        shuffleDeck(sourceOwner, ctx.rng);
        break;
      }
    }

    // rules-reference.md §12: searching the deck always reshuffles afterward, unconditionally --
    // including when only the discard pile was actually searched (same as Junjertrug Horn).
    shuffleDeck(ctx.controllingPlayer, ctx.rng);
  },
});
