// Shared pre-combat die-roll consumer (Bucket D Tier 2 completion 2026-08-14 -- Fate Whimsy
// final 4 cards rebuild). NOT a card file -- registers ONE shared roll consumer, keyed
// `'preCombatDieRoll'`, used by every card whose printed roll happens "whenever [self]
// attacks/is attacked" and used to fire from a synchronous combat-body hook (Grass Hyren
// Evade, Kintor Snowblind, Sun Glasses Glare).
//
// The mechanism (see `registry.ts`'s `offersPreCombatDieRoll` doc comment and `combat.ts`'s
// `previewPreCombatDieRolls` for the full picture):
//
//   1. Play.tsx's pre-combat resolution chain (between preCombatTargetChoices and springOut)
//      queries `previewPreCombatDieRolls(attackerId, defenderId)` for the list of cards that
//      want a Whimsy-eligible pre-combat roll.
//   2. For each returning card, Play.tsx calls `requestDieRoll(state, cardDb, card, ..., 6,
//      'preCombatDieRoll', {cardInstanceId: card.instanceId}, rng)`.
//   3. `requestDieRoll` either fast-paths (no Whimsy reactor eligible -- consumer fires inline)
//      or arms `state.pendingDieRoll` for the standard Whimsy window.
//   4. When the roll finalizes, THIS consumer fires. It stores the final roll on
//      `state.preCombatDieRollAnswers[cardInstanceId]`.
//   5. Play.tsx polls the answers map, collects each card's roll as it lands, then threads the
//      accumulated map into `AttackDeclaration.preCombatDieRolls` before calling `declareAttack`.
//   6. Inside `declareAttack`, the affected card's onAttack/onBeforeEnergyRemoved hook reads
//      `ctx.scratch.preCombatDieRolls?.[self.instanceId]` and uses it INSTEAD of an inline
//      `rollDie(ctx.rng, ...)`. Missing entry -> fall back to inline roll (bot mode / test
//      callers who don't pre-collect -- Whimsy simply doesn't fire in that path).
//
// FALLBACK NOTE: this consumer is a NO-OP under one specific race: if the same card wins two
// pre-combat rolls for the SAME attack (which can't happen today -- one card, one hook, one
// roll per attack), the second overwrite is by design. If a future card needs multiple
// pre-combat rolls per attack, extend the payload with a `slotKey` to disambiguate.

import { registerRollConsumer } from '../rollConsumers.js';

registerRollConsumer('preCombatDieRoll', (state, _cardDb, _source, payload, finalRoll) => {
  const { cardInstanceId } = payload as { cardInstanceId: string };
  state.preCombatDieRollAnswers[cardInstanceId] = finalRoll;
});
