// The Hunt (Weave Spell, cost 1) -- "All of your Weave Creatures that can attack may attack twice
// this turn."
//
// Weave Spell pass (2026-07-26), Category A. oneShotBonusAttacks is the existing extra-attack
// primitive (Ayebaw's Double Strike precedent), counted against the per-turn budget by combat.ts.
//
// *** THE ONE JUDGMENT CALL IN THE task #557 FAMILY -- READ BEFORE CHANGING ANYTHING HERE. ***
//
// This card has the SHAPE of the bug task #557 fixed: a Spell that sweeps the board once and writes
// a counter, so a Weave Creature entering play later this turn gets nothing. For Yaki, Yaki's
// Gauntlets, Korremar Avatar and Swamp Moga that was wrong and they became standing
// `grantsBonusAttacksTo` / `grantsAttackEnergyBonusTo` grants. The Hunt was deliberately LEFT as a
// one-shot, on one word of its text: "all of your Weave Creatures THAT CAN ATTACK". The restrictive
// clause selects a set at resolve time -- a Creature not yet in play cannot attack, so it is not in
// the set. The four cards that changed have no such clause; they say "each of your Creatures"
// (unqualified) or "until the end of your turn", which describe a window, not a set.
//
// That reading is defensible but it is NOT certain, and it is the only thing keeping this card in a
// different bucket from its four siblings. If a judge ruling, an errata, or a playtest report ever
// says a Creature played after The Hunt resolves SHOULD get the extra attack, the fix is mechanical:
// delete this resolveSpell body and replace it with a `grantsBonusAttacksTo` that checks a
// duration tag set at resolve time -- copy yakisGauntlets.ts verbatim, swapping Naroom for Weave.
// The infrastructure is already there; nothing new needs building.
//
// "that can attack" is deliberately NOT re-checked here. A Creature currently unable to attack
// (frozen, restricted, already attacked) simply never spends the bonus, and evaluating attack
// legality at Spell-resolution time would freeze a judgement that combat.ts re-derives properly at
// declaration time -- and would be wrong the moment the restriction lifts later in the same turn.

import { registerCardEffect } from './registry.js';
import { creaturesControlledBy, effectiveRegions } from '../targeting.js';

registerCardEffect('The Hunt', {
  resolveSpell: (ctx) => {
    for (const creature of creaturesControlledBy(ctx.state, ctx.cardDb, ctx.controllingPlayer.playerId)) {
      if (effectiveRegions(ctx.state, ctx.cardDb, creature).includes('Weave')) {
        creature.oneShotBonusAttacks += 1;
      }
    }
  },
});
